{
 'title': 'Henry Dunant and the birth of the Red Cross', 'context': 'A lecture in a history course about how the Red Cross movement began.',
 'vocab': ['battlefield', 'wounded', 'neutral', 'convention', 'emblem'],
 'script': [
  ('Lecturer', 'US', "Good morning, everyone. Today we're going to look at the beginnings of one of the largest volunteer organisations in the world: the Red Cross. Its story begins with one man and one terrible day."),
  ('Lecturer', 'US', "The man was Henry Dunant, who was born in Geneva, in Switzerland, in 1828. He was a businessman, and in the eighteen-fifties he had business interests in Algeria, in North Africa. In June 1859, he travelled to northern Italy, hoping to meet the French emperor, Napoleon the Third, to ask for help with his business."),
  ('Lecturer', 'US', "At that time, France and its ally were fighting the Austrian army in northern Italy. On the evening of the twenty-fourth of June, Dunant arrived near the small town of Solferino, just after a huge battle had taken place there. Around forty thousand soldiers had been killed or wounded in a single day. What shocked Dunant most was that thousands of wounded men were simply left lying on the battlefield, because the armies had very few doctors and almost no medical supplies."),
  ('Lecturer', 'US', "Dunant forgot all about his business. He went to the nearby town of Castiglione, where many of the wounded had been taken, and organised the local people, many of them women, to help. They brought water and food, washed and dressed wounds, and wrote letters for dying soldiers to their families. Importantly, Dunant insisted that they should care for soldiers from both sides equally. The local women repeated a phrase in Italian which means 'all are brothers'."),
  ('Lecturer', 'US', "When Dunant returned to Geneva, he couldn't forget what he had seen. In 1862, he wrote a short book called A Memory of Solferino. In it, he described the battle and its terrible results, and he made two important suggestions. First, every country should set up a society of trained volunteers who would be ready to care for wounded soldiers in times of war. And second, countries should sign an international agreement to protect these volunteers and the wounded."),
  ('Lecturer', 'US', "The book was a great success and was translated into many languages. In 1863, Dunant and four other men from Geneva formed a committee to put his ideas into action. This group later became the International Committee of the Red Cross. Just one year later, in 1864, the representatives of twelve countries signed the first Geneva Convention, which said that medical staff and the wounded must be protected and treated as neutral."),
  ('Lecturer', 'US', "The volunteers needed a symbol, so that soldiers would recognise and respect them. They chose a red cross on a white background. This is the Swiss flag with the colours reversed, as a way of honouring Switzerland, where the movement began. Later, in the eighteen-seventies, during a war with Russia, the Ottoman Empire began to use a red crescent instead, and today the Red Crescent is used in many countries."),
  ('Lecturer', 'US', "Sadly, Dunant's own life did not go well. His business in Algeria failed, and in 1867 he went bankrupt. He had to leave Geneva, and for many years he lived in poverty, almost forgotten. Then, in 1895, a journalist found him living in a small care home in the Swiss village of Heiden and wrote an article about him. People around the world were reminded of what he had done, and in 1901, Dunant was awarded the very first Nobel Peace Prize, which he shared with a French campaigner for peace."),
  ('Lecturer', 'US', "Today, the Red Cross and Red Crescent Movement has national societies in more than one hundred and ninety countries, and it depends on millions of volunteers. Every year, on the eighth of May, World Red Cross and Red Crescent Day is celebrated. That date was chosen because it was Dunant's birthday. Next time, we'll look at how the movement's work has changed since the two World Wars."),
 ],
 'groups': [
  G('Questions 31–40. Complete the notes below. Write ONE WORD ONLY for each answer.', [
   Q('note', 'Dunant worked as a __________', 'businessman', 'He was a businessman', 'He was a businessman.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'In 1859 he hoped to meet the French __________', 'emperor', 'hoping to meet the French emperor', 'He wanted to meet the emperor.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Armies at Solferino had very few doctors and almost no medical __________', 'supplies', 'the armies had very few doctors and almost no medical supplies', 'There were no medical supplies.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Local people also wrote __________ for dying soldiers', 'letters', 'wrote letters for dying soldiers to their families', 'They wrote letters.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Dunant\'s 1862 __________ described the battle', 'book', 'he wrote a short book called A Memory of Solferino', 'He wrote a book.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'He suggested societies of trained __________ in every country', 'volunteers', 'every country should set up a society of trained volunteers', 'He wanted trained volunteers.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Under the 1864 agreement, medical staff were to be treated as __________', 'neutral', 'must be protected and treated as neutral', 'They were treated as neutral.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'The red cross symbol is the reverse of the Swiss __________', 'flag', 'This is the Swiss flag with the colours reversed', 'It reverses the Swiss flag.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'In 1895, a __________ found Dunant in a care home', 'journalist', 'a journalist found him living in a small care home', 'A journalist found him.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'World Red Cross Day takes place in __________', 'May', 'on the eighth of May', 'It is on the eighth of May.', skill='dates', max=1),
  ]),
 ],
},
